  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain will occur tonight into Wednesday morning,\nwith showers and thunderstorms Wednesday afternoon. There is a \nmarginal risk for severe thunderstorms Wednesday afternoon and \nevening. Locally heavy rainfall is possible which could lead to \nminor urban flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure lifting across Quebec province will begin to draw a \nwarm front across the region tonight. Overrunning rain will \nexpand across the region early tonight then persist through the \nremainder of the night. Precipitable water values increase to \naround 2 inches Downeast tonight, with 1.50 to 1.75 inches \nacross northern areas. A strong low level jet will also cross \nDowneast areas overnight. Elevated instability will also support\nisolated/scattered thunderstorms overnight. With the higher \nprecipitable water values and support from the low level jet, \nexpect the heavier rain totals tonight will occur Downeast. The \nwarm front will exit the region Wednesday morning, with the \nsteadier rain ending in the wake of the warm front. The warm \nsector will cross the region Wednesday afternoon, with a cold \nfront starTing to cross late. Showers and thunderstorms will \ndevelop in the warm sector Wednesday afternoon in advance of the\ncold front. A strong mid/upper level jet will help provide \nsupport for the thunderstorms. Additional support will be provided\nfrom a disturbance in advance of an approaching upper trof. The\nstrength of afternoon thunderstorms will largely depend on the \namount of heating and de-stabilization which occurs in the warm \nsector with greater sun leading to increased instability. Strong\nto possibly severe thunderstorms are possible Wednesday \nafternoon if enough instability can develop. CAPE values of \n1000-2000 J/kg, with Downdraft CAPE values of 1000-1500 J/kg \nare expected Wednesday afternoon dependent on the degree of\nde-stabilization. The region remains in a Marginal Risk for \nsevere thunderstorms Wednesday. Showers/thunderstorms will then \nend in the wake of the cold front Wednesday night. Rainfall \ntotals tonight into Wednesday night will generally range from \naround 3/4 to 1.50 inches Downeast, with around 1/3 to 3/4 of an\ninch north. However, locally greater totals are possible \nparticularly with any convection. This could lead to localized \nurban/poor-drainage flooding, particularly Downeast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dangerous Rip Currents and potentially High \nSurf along the Downeast coast Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nRough seas will occur along the Downeast coast Wednesday with\nlarge breaking waves from a long period southerly swell. A High\nSurf Advisory could be needed along the Downeast coast \nWednesday dependent on how rapidly seas build.\n\nOn Thursday, seas will be about 2 feet lower as compared to \nWednesday. That said, they will still be rather rough for this \ntime of year, with 4-6 ft seas still mostly as a south swell \nwith a fairly long period of around 9 seconds. So although seas \nwill be a bit calmer, conditions along the coast could be \nhazardous for people recreating near the shore. Also, the \nweather Thursday will be quite a bit nicer than Wednesday, and\nthis will bring more people outside and in potential harm's \nway.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Potential for a system moving in Monday/Tuesday,\nbringing some wetting rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nQuiet, dry weather from Thursday to Sunday with a warming trend.\nThen Monday/Tuesday, most models bring wet weather as a low \npressure system moves northeast through the region. Exact track \nand timing of the low pressure system is uncertain, but in \ngeneral, models favor rain at some point Monday/Tuesday, with a \nchance of thunderstorms too.",
